WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.260
<v Instructor>So now let's talk</v>

2
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.900
about Amazon EC2 Spot Instances.

3
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.550
So we know that with Spot Instances,

4
00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.633
we can get a discount of up to 90%

5
00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:10.020
compared to On-demand instances.

6
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.610
How does it work to get a Spot Instance?

7
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.870
Well, you're going to define

8
00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.570
a max spot price you're willing to pay

9
00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.290
and you will get the EC2 Instance, the Spot Instance,

10
00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.210
as long as the current spot price

11
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.210
is below the max you are willing to spend.

12
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.950
So this hourly spot price will vary

13
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.300
based on offer and capacity.

14
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.250
And then, we'll show you this in the next slide.

15
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.910
And so, if the current spot price goes over your max price,

16
00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.830
then you can choose to either stop

17
00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.300
or terminate your instance

18
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.880
and you have a two minutes grace period.

19
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.250
So based on your workload and your application,

20
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.630
as soon as you have notification

21
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.210
that you're about to be stopped or terminated,

22
00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.130
you need to do some shut down,

23
00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.540
some saving of some documents,

24
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.570
saving of data to stop serving traffic as well

25
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.910
to make sure that you can gracefully shut down

26
00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.010
and that your workload is adapted

27
00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.230
to this kind of situations.

28
00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.560
So Spot Instances are best used for batch jobs,

29
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.170
data analysis, or workloads that are, of course,

30
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.280
are resilient to failures

31
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.320
because you will get stopping

32
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.090
and termination of your instances.

33
00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:13.110
It's definitely not great for critical jobs or databases.

34
00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.290
Now, let's have a look at the Spot Instance Pricing.

35
00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.550
So this is a graph that I obtained

36
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.920
directly from the AWS console.

37
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.470
And as we can see here, this gives us the price

38
00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.780
for a m4.large instance

39
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.330
over three months in the spot zone.

40
00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:28.470
So as you can see,

41
00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.532
you have six lines for us-east-1a.

42
00:01:31.532 --> 00:01:32.790
One, sorry, the region.

43
00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.050
And you have six availability zones.

44
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:34.950
So, six prices.

45
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:35.940
So, as you can see,

46
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.502
the spot price does vary

47
00:01:37.502 --> 00:01:40.230
based on the AZ you're considering.

48
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.780
And so as we can see, over three months,

49
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.340
it has varied quite a bit.

50
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.170
For example, if you look at that yellow line,

51
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.590
it has varied between 0.4 to 0.45

52
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.030
all the way down to almost 0.35,

53
00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.850
let's say, and so on.

54
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.760
And so let's assume that we are defining

55
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.320
a user-defined max price

56
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.370
to be this black horizontal bar dotted line, okay?

57
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.730
And so what we'd see is that if the yellow line

58
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.070
is above the dotted line,

59
00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.421
then the current spot price

60
00:02:09.421 --> 00:02:12.330
is going to be more than the max price we have defined.

61
00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.069
And so in this instance, right here,

62
00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:16.350
we're going to lose our Spot Instance.

63
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.480
So we have to terminate it or stop it.

64
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.869
But if the price goes down,

65
00:02:19.869 --> 00:02:23.760
so if the yellow line goes down our dotted line,

66
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.969
that means that we are willing to pay that price.

67
00:02:25.969 --> 00:02:29.310
And so, we are gaining that Spot Instance in this meantime.

68
00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.410
So this is how we can optimize the cost

69
00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.630
of our workload very easily.

70
00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:34.710
And so as you can see,

71
00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:38.820
the On-demand price is $0.10 per hour.

72
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.200
So that's really, really cheap.

73
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.290
And then, the Spot Instance price is even cheaper.

74
00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.870
It's around 0.4, I would say.

75
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.200
So it's a 60% saving in that instance

76
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.730
for the Spot Instance Pricing.

77
00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:51.720
So, lots of savings.

78
00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.670
And obviously, if you were to define

79
00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.230
your user-defined max price

80
00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:59.250
to be something like $0.05 per hour,

81
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.460
then no Spot Instances will be reclaimed

82
00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.570
because you're always willing to pay more

83
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.610
than the actual Spot Instance price.

84
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.460
So no one can predict how the price goes over time.

85
00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.470
But as you can see, it can be rather stable,

86
00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:11.700
it can fluctuate a little bit.

87
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.510
But overall, it still provides you huge savings

88
00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.190
compared to On-demand, okay?

89
00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:20.460
Okay, next, how do you terminate a Spot Instance?

90
00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.580
So we have to first understand how a spot request works.

91
00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.070
And so for this, let's consider a spot request.

92
00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.620
So with a spot request, you are defining

93
00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.630
how many instances you want,

94
00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.700
your maximum price you're going to pay,

95
00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:33.810
the launch specification,

96
00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.310
so the AMI and so on.

97
00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.980
And when your request is valid from and until,

98
00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.300
but it could be infinite.

99
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.860
And then, the request type,

100
00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:41.940
and that's very important to understand.

101
00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.620
There's two types of requests.

102
00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.530
You can do one-time request for Spot Instances

103
00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:49.500
or a persistent request for persistent instances.

104
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.570
So let's have a look at the difference.

105
00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:53.370
So if it's a one-time request,

106
00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.250
as soon as your spot request is fulfilled,

107
00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.320
your instances are going to be launched.

108
00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.170
And then, your spot request will go away

109
00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.240
because it was a one-time request type.

110
00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.073
So in this case,

111
00:04:04.073 --> 00:04:07.200
your spot request is going to go away and you're fine.

112
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.450
But if it's as persistent request type,

113
00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:10.740
that means that we want

114
00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.990
this number of instances to be valid

115
00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:17.010
as long as the spot request is valid from to valid until.

116
00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.660
And so that means that if, somehow,

117
00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.390
your instances do get stopped, okay?

118
00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.670
Or interrupted based on the spot price,

119
00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.640
then your spot request will go back into action,

120
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.701
and when things can be validated,

121
00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.890
will restart Spot Instances for you.

122
00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:35.910
So if somehow you stop a Spot Instance in persistent mode

123
00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.070
and your spot request is still active,

124
00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.770
your spot request automatically will be smart enough

125
00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.350
to restart a launch and instance for you.

126
00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.565
And this is something you can also see in this diagram

127
00:04:45.565 --> 00:04:47.610
that I won't spend too much time on it.

128
00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:48.930
So what you understand out of it

129
00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:53.280
is that if you want to cancel a spot request,

130
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.350
it needs to be in the open state,

131
00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.300
the active state or the disabled state.

132
00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.830
That means that basically it's not failed,

133
00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.350
it's not canceled or it's not closed, okay?

134
00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:04.557
And when you want to cancel a spot request,

135
00:05:04.557 --> 00:05:06.060
so if you take the spot request

136
00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.890
on the left hand side and you cancel it,

137
00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.160
it's not going to terminate any instances

138
00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.600
that you have launched from before.

139
00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.910
So it is still your responsibility

140
00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:16.680
to terminate these instances

141
00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.770
and not the responsibility of AWS.

142
00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.900
So as such, if you want to terminate

143
00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.080
Spot Instances for good and not have them relaunched,

144
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.170
you need to first cancel the spot request.

145
00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:32.100
And then, you terminate the associated Spot Instances.

146
00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.880
Because if you were to terminate the Spot Instances first,

147
00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:37.980
remember it goes back into the spot request

148
00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.060
and the spot request says:

149
00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.620
okay, you wanted six instances,

150
00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.270
but I can see you have zero right now.

151
00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.640
I'm going to launch six instances for you.

152
00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.740
So, again, the right order to cancel

153
00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.720
and to terminate Spot Instances

154
00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.315
is to first cancel the spot request

155
00:05:50.315 --> 00:05:54.210
to make sure that no new instances will be launched by AWS.

156
00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.660
And then, you terminate the associated Spot Instances.

157
00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:58.890
And I'm spending a bit of time on this

158
00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.672
because this is something that can come up in the exam.

159
00:06:01.672 --> 00:06:03.810
Okay, next: Spot Fleets.

160
00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:06.450
So this is the ultimate way to save money.

161
00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:08.820
So Spot Fleets is going to be a way to define

162
00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.210
for you to get a set of Spot Instances

163
00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:15.090
and optionally On-demand instances.

164
00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:16.290
That's why it's called a fleet.

165
00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.050
And the Spot Fleet will try its best

166
00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.610
to meet the target capacity

167
00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.800
with the price constraints you defined.

168
00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.010
So it will launch from possible launch pools.

169
00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:29.070
So it can have different instance types, different OS,

170
00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.630
and availability zones.

171
00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.720
And so you're going to define multiple launch pools.

172
00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.220
So multiple instance, multiple everything.

173
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:38.400
And then, the fleet will choose the best

174
00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.610
and most appropriate launch pool for you.

175
00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.180
And when your Spot Fleet either reaches your budget

176
00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.160
or reaches the capacity you wanted,

177
00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.830
then it will stop launching instances.

178
00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:52.020
And so, you define a strategy

179
00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.257
to allocate Spot Instances in your Spot Fleet,

180
00:06:54.257 --> 00:06:56.040
and that's something to remember.

181
00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.666
The first one is lowestPrice,

182
00:06:57.666 --> 00:07:00.060
and that's gonna be some the most popular one

183
00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:01.260
going into the exam,

184
00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:05.130
which is that the Spot Fleet will launch instances

185
00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.010
from the pool that has the lowest price

186
00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.380
and that gives you a lot of cost optimization.

187
00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:11.730
And this is a great option

188
00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.980
if you have a very short workload.

189
00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.170
You can also have a diversified way

190
00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:17.940
to launch your Spot Instances,

191
00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.643
in which case, the launch,

192
00:07:19.643 --> 00:07:22.380
the Spot Instances will be distributed

193
00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.410
across all the pools that you have defined from before,

194
00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.840
which is great for availability and long workloads.

195
00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.580
Because if one pool goes away,

196
00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:32.190
then your other pools are still active.

197
00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:33.630
Then, you have capacityOptimized.

198
00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.970
So you have a pool with the optimal capacity

199
00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.100
for the number of instances you want.

200
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.230
And then, you have priceCapacityOptimized,

201
00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:41.580
which is first choosing the pool

202
00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:43.710
with the highest capacity available.

203
00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:45.240
And then, select within that

204
00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.740
the one that has the lowest price,

205
00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.800
which is the best choice for most workloads.

206
00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.530
So the idea is that Spot Fleets can be complicated,

207
00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.530
but what you have to remember is that using the Spot Fleet,

208
00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.510
you're able to define multiple launch pools,

209
00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.500
multiple instance types

210
00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.330
until you're just interesting in raw power.

211
00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:03.510
And then if you use the lowest price discounts

212
00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:06.090
at the lowest price price strategy for Spot Fleets,

213
00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.329
then the Spot Fleets will automatically request

214
00:08:08.329 --> 00:08:11.499
the spot instances with the lowest price for us.

215
00:08:11.499 --> 00:08:15.120
And so, Spot Fleet gives us an extra saving

216
00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.610
based on Spot Instances because it's smart enough

217
00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.920
to choose the right Spot Instance pool

218
00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:22.620
to allow us to get the maximum amount of savings.

219
00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:25.350
And this is the benefits of Spot Fleets.

220
00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.330
And, again, you need to understand the difference here

221
00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:30.690
of doing a very simple Spot Instance request

222
00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.760
where you know exactly the type of instance you want

223
00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:35.730
and the AZ you want to using a Spot Fleet and saying,

224
00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.410
okay, you can choose all these instance types

225
00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:39.930
in all these AZ, and what I need from you

226
00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:43.020
is to give me the lowest price, for example, okay?

227
00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:44.880
So that's hopefully is giving you

228
00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:46.442
a lot of insights into how Spot Fleets

229
00:08:46.442 --> 00:08:48.480
and Spot Instances are different.

230
00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:49.500
I hope you like this lecture

231
00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.450
and I will see you in the next lecture.

